WEBVTT

00:00:03.096 --> 00:00:06.526
Here’s how to run the exercise “Explain it to a friend”.

00:00:06.576 --> 00:00:08.866
It takes about twenty-five minutes.

00:00:08.916 --> 00:00:10.726
Start by setting the scene.

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:15.466
Say something like: “Imagine you’re at dinner with a good friend who doesn’t work here.

00:00:15.516 --> 00:00:20.186
They ask: so what does your team actually do, and why does it matter?

00:00:20.236 --> 00:00:22.746
Explain it the way you would at that table.”

00:00:22.796 --> 00:00:27.206
Put people in pairs. One person explains, for about four minutes.

00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:34.486
The other person listens, and does one thing only: writes down phrases that sound clear, human and meaningful.

00:00:34.536 --> 00:00:36.226
One phrase per post-it.

00:00:36.276 --> 00:00:39.446
The listener doesn’t correct, debate or help.

00:00:39.496 --> 00:00:41.126
Then they switch roles.

00:00:41.176 --> 00:00:42.986
Walk around while they talk.

00:00:43.036 --> 00:00:48.738
If you hear people sliding into presentation mode, nudge them: “Would you say it like that to your friend?”

00:00:48.788 --> 00:00:50.758
Then bring everyone together.

00:00:50.808 --> 00:00:54.838
Ask the pairs to put their post-its on the big canvas and read them out.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:58.338
Now cluster them: which phrases say similar things?

00:00:58.388 --> 00:01:00.318
Which words keep coming back?

00:01:00.368 --> 00:01:02.178
Circle the strongest ones.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:07.138
You’ll notice that the language is warmer and more concrete than anything you’d write in a meeting.

00:01:07.188 --> 00:01:12.758
Now draft the statement together: “We exist to… for… by…”

00:01:12.808 --> 00:01:14.398
Use the circled words.

00:01:14.448 --> 00:01:16.038
Don’t try to make it perfect.

00:01:16.088 --> 00:01:22.838
If the group gets stuck on wording, ask two volunteers to polish it during the break, and bring back two versions to choose from.

00:01:22.888 --> 00:01:27.418
If you have time, add the second exercise: “In three years’ time…”

00:01:27.468 --> 00:01:30.418
Everyone writes two or three endings to that sentence.

00:01:30.468 --> 00:01:33.558
Group them, then give everyone three dots to vote.

00:01:33.608 --> 00:01:37.558
Ask them to vote for the version that feels true, not just ambitious.

00:01:37.608 --> 00:01:40.778
Then name two or three signs that would show it’s becoming real.

